      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The documentation is a fascinating mix, leaning heavily on oral tradition, yet with scattered, tantalizing glimpses in written accounts. It is rare to find a single, pristine travel journal from that era that explicitly labels a creature as a \"Sand-Whisperer\" or \"Crag-Rascal.\" Most of the detailed taxonomies you're hearing about are preserved within local songs, cautionary tales, and the specialized knowledge of shamans or elders—the very oral traditions that survive when formal record-keeping falters.\n\nHowever, when travelers did write, they often used coded language or generalized descriptions that map perfectly onto these types. A journal might note, \"The camp was disturbed by unseen hands and the beasts grew restless,\" which, to a local, immediately signaled the presence of a Crag-Rascal. Similarly, descriptions of inexplicable mirages or strange, localized wind patterns in the desert often correlate with the attributed actions of the Sand-Whisperers.\n\nSo, while you might not find a detailed field guide, the *effects* of these entities are often recorded in the most pragmatic historical documents—the logs detailing lost livestock, unexplained delays, or strange localized weather phenomena. It's as if the written record documents the *",
